#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" :question/math-academy-id "e-19814" :question/problem "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$, where $x_1 < x_2$. What is the value of $x_1 + x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} + 2; y = 3x\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2 &= 3x \\\\\nx^{2} - 3x + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "9aa14c81-aeaa-51b7-9d16-468e22bc113d" :question/math-academy-id "e-19815" :question/problem "The parabola $y = -x^2 + x + 4$ and the line $y = 2x + 2$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$, where $x_1 < x_2$. What is the value of $y_1 + y_2$?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y =-x^{2} + x + 4; y = 2x + 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + x + 4 &= 2x + 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} - x + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} + x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n- (x + 2)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = -2$ and $x_2 = 1$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either the curve or the line. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n$y|= 2x + 2; = 2(-2) + 2; =-2$,\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-2)$.\n- Substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x + 2; = 2(1) + 2; = 4$,\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,4)$.\n\nThe points of intersection are $(-2,-2)$ and $(1,4)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_1 = -2,\\; y_2 = 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = -2 + 4 = 2\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da3cbc81f8170e3cf098a97d249e3ad8f0abf358fa641d8b94b1eb301f1a7858.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19816" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q)$. What is $p+q$?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y =-x^{2} + 6x; y = 9\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 6x &= 9 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} - 6x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 3)(x - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3$.\n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9)$. Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9$, and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/id #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-19817" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3$, what is the range of possible values of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=x +2k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2-x+3\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} - x + 3; y = x + 2k\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - x + 3 &= x + 2k \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 3 - 2k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with\n\n$$\na=1,\\, b=-2\n$$\n\nand $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(3 - 2k)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{4 - 12 + 8k}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{8k - 8}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8k - 8 & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < 8 \\\\ k & < 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt 1$."}]}
